---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Callout from '../components/Callout.astro';
import FeedbackWidget from '../components/FeedbackWidget.astro';

const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, '');

const toc = [
  { id: 'model', text: '1. Mental Model' },
  { id: 'transfer', text: '2. Basic Transfer' },
  { id: 'two-edicts', text: '3. Two Edicts & Change' },
  { id: 'split', text: '4. Splitting Balances' },
  { id: 'mint', text: '5. Minting Runes' },
  { id: 'etch', text: '6. Etching a Rune' },
  { id: 'etch-fails', text: '7. How Etchings Fail' },
  { id: 'burn', text: '8. Burning Runes' },
  { id: 'review', text: '9. Review Checklist' },
  { id: 'support', text: '10. Protocol Support' },
];
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Step-by-Step Guide to Runes"
  description="Practical, worked examples demonstrating transfers, multi-rune edicts, balance splits, open mints, and etchings on Bitcoin."
  currentRoute="guide.html"
  kicker="Worked Tutorial"
  toc={toc}
>
  <div class="status-line">
    Practical worked walkthroughs with exact hex scripts and byte breakdowns.
  </div>

  <h2 id="model">1. Mental Model</h2>
  <p>
    When working with Runes, remember that runes are not stored inside the runestone itself.
    The runestone is merely an instruction sheet telling the Bitcoin transaction how to route the runes that entered through the spent inputs.
  </p>

  <h2 id="transfer">2. Basic Transfer</h2>
  <p>
    Suppose you have a UTXO containing 1,000 units of rune <code>840000:3</code>.
    You want to send all 1,000 units to a recipient at output 1.
  </p>
  <p>
    The simplest way is to omit all edicts and set the <strong>Pointer</strong> (Tag 22) to 1:
  </p>
  <pre><code>OP_RETURN OP_13 OP_PUSHBYTES_2 16 01</code></pre>
  <p>
    <code>16</code> is varint 22 (Pointer), and <code>01</code> is output index 1.
    All unallocated runes entering the transaction route directly to output 1.
  </p>

  <h2 id="two-edicts">3. Two Edicts & Change</h2>
  <p>
    Suppose you hold 5,000 units of rune <code>840000:3</code>. You want to send 2,000 units to Alice (output 1), 1,000 units to Bob (output 2), and return the remaining 2,000 units to yourself (output 3).
  </p>
  <p>
    You construct two edicts and set the pointer to your change output:
  </p>
  <ul style="padding-left: 1.5rem; margin-bottom: 1.5rem;">
    <li>Edict 1: <code>840000:3</code>, amount 2,000 -> output 1</li>
    <li>Edict 2: <code>840000:3</code>, amount 1,000 -> output 2</li>
    <li>Pointer: Output 3 (receives the remaining 2,000 units)</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="split">4. Splitting Balances</h2>
  <p>
    Under Rule R38, if an edict specifies an output index equal to the total number of transaction outputs, the specified amount is divided across all non-OP_RETURN outputs:
  </p>
  <ul style="padding-left: 1.5rem; margin-bottom: 1.5rem;">
    <li>If amount is 0: The entire unallocated balance divides evenly across all spendable outputs.</li>
    <li>If amount is non-zero: Units are distributed in order to each spendable output until the balance runs out.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="mint">5. Minting Runes</h2>
  <p>
    To mint an active rune, include Tag 20 with the target Rune ID:
  </p>
  <pre><code>OP_RETURN OP_13 OP_PUSHBYTES_6 14 c0 a2 33 14 03</code></pre>
  <p>
    Tag 20 appears twice: first for the block height (840,000), then for the transaction index (3).
    The minted units enter the unallocated pool and route to the pointer or first spendable output.
  </p>

  <h2 id="etch">6. Etching a Rune</h2>
  <p>
    Etching requires two transactions:
  </p>
  <ol style="padding-left: 1.5rem; margin-bottom: 1.5rem;">
    <li><strong>Commitment:</strong> A Taproot output containing the little-endian bytes of the name.</li>
    <li><strong>Reveal:</strong> After 6 blocks, spend the commitment output with a runestone containing Tag 2 (Flags: Etching) and Tag 4 (Rune name value).</li>
  </ol>

  <h2 id="etch-fails">7. How Etchings Fail</h2>
  <p>
    Common reasons etchings do not succeed as intended:
  </p>
  <ul style="padding-left: 1.5rem; margin-bottom: 1.5rem;">
    <li><strong>Too Young:</strong> Spending the commitment before 6 confirmations (causes name to be disregarded and assigned a reserved sequence name).</li>
    <li><strong>Locked Name:</strong> Etching a name with fewer letters than unlocked at the current block height (silently disregarded).</li>
    <li><strong>Already Etched:</strong> Name was etched earlier by another transaction (silently disregarded).</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="burn">8. Burning Runes</h2>
  <p>
    Runes can be deliberately burned by explicitly directing an edict to an <code>OP_RETURN</code> output.
    Any units directed to an <code>OP_RETURN</code> output are permanently destroyed.
  </p>

  <h2 id="review">9. Review Checklist</h2>
  <ul style="padding-left: 1.5rem; margin-bottom: 1.5rem;">
    <li>[ ] Confirm output indices match planned addresses.</li>
    <li>[ ] Check that pointer output index is strictly less than total outputs.</li>
    <li>[ ] Verify total script size is under 83 bytes for fast standard relay.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="support">10. Protocol Support</h2>
  <p>
    For interactive experiments and testing, launch the <a href={`${base}/studio.html`}>Runestone Studio</a> or test your scripts in the <a href={`${base}/decoder.html`}>Standalone Decoder</a>.
  </p>

  <FeedbackWidget pageTitle="Step-by-Step Guide to Runes" />
</BaseLayout>
